;; FH-BEHAVIOR-002 — the use site is DATA, and it owns exactly one node.
;;
;; The declaration is registered code; the use site is the value that names it.
;; Everything below defends that split, because it is what lets a structural
;; test, a tool and a reader see what a node's behavior is without holding the
;; implementation:
;;
;;   * the option roster is closed to `:use` / `:target` / `:config`;
;;   * `:use` names a REGISTERED behavior, so an id nothing answers to is
;;     refused rather than mounting a node nothing will connect to;
;;   * `:config` is data at every depth — a callback, a node or a host instance
;;     inside it would be a use site the tree cannot record and a test cannot
;;     read, so it is refused on BOTH hosts rather than only where the tree
;;     happens to be built;
;;   * the boundary decorates exactly ONE element, because a behavior owns one
;;     node and a group is not a node.
;;
;; Every case names a declaration in `re-frame.freehand.behavior-views` and the
;; outcome rendering it must produce — `:renders`, or the `:rf.error/id` the
;; refusal must carry. The views are rendered on both hosts, so acceptance and
;; refusal are one answer rather than two that agree.
{:fh/id "FH-BEHAVIOR-002"
 :fh/law
 "A `[v/behavior {…} node]` use site is data with a closed option roster: `:use` must name a registered behavior, `:config` is refused if it carries a non-data value at any depth, and the boundary decorates exactly one element — a second child, a declared view, a fragment or text is refused, as are Freehand children under an opaque behavior."

 :option-keys [:use :target :config]

 :cases
 [{:note "the ordinary attachment — id, semantic target, public config"
   :view :plain :outcome :renders}

  {:note "a behavior nothing commands needs no target"
   :view :no-target :outcome :renders}

  {:note "the `:layout` arm is the same use site with a different registration"
   :view :layout-timed :outcome :renders}

  {:note "an opaque behavior over an empty node"
   :view :opaque-host :outcome :renders}

  {:note "two occurrences under distinct semantic ids"
   :view :pair :outcome :renders}

  {:note "an option outside the closed roster is refused — by the boundary's own
          CLOSED props schema, which is where a published view states its roster
          once as data (FH-PROPS-005); the behavior door's roster check is the
          same closure said again, and is what answers for an unpublished caller"
   :view :unknown-option :outcome :rf.error/view-bad-props}

  {:note "`:use` is required — there is no default behavior"
   :view :missing-use :outcome :rf.error/behavior-bad-args}

  {:note "an id no declaration registered is refused at the use site, on both hosts"
   :view :unregistered-use :outcome :rf.error/behavior-bad-args}

  {:note "`:config` is the public configuration and is a map"
   :view :config-not-map :outcome :rf.error/behavior-bad-args}

  {:note "a callback NESTED inside the config is refused — the rule holds at every depth"
   :view :config-carries-a-callback :outcome :rf.error/behavior-bad-args}

  {:note "a behavior owns ONE node, so a second child is refused"
   :view :two-children :outcome :rf.error/behavior-bad-args}

  {:note "a declared view is a boundary, not a node"
   :view :view-child :outcome :rf.error/behavior-bad-args}

  {:note "a fragment is a group, not a node"
   :view :fragment-child :outcome :rf.error/behavior-bad-args}

  {:note "and text is a value, not a node"
   :view :text-child :outcome :rf.error/behavior-bad-args}

  {:note "an opaque behavior owns the descendants, so declared children are refused
          rather than rendered and silently overwritten"
   :view :opaque-with-children :outcome :rf.error/behavior-bad-args}

  {:note "the COMPILED twin of the empty opaque host renders — the positive control
          the discriminating opaque-child case is measured against (rf2-drpa3.127)"
   :view :opaque-host-compiled :outcome :renders}

  {:note "and the COMPILED twin of the opaque-with-children host refuses on the same
          diagnostic — the build cannot prove opacity (a registered fact), so the
          compiled marker enforces the opaque-child law at render exactly as the
          interpreted twin does. JVM, interpreted browser and compiled browser agree
          (rf2-drpa3.127)"
   :view :opaque-with-children-compiled :outcome :rf.error/behavior-bad-args}]}
